                <pb facs="0144" n="48" sameAs="frus:frus1898/pg48"/>

                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Proceedings held by order of the imperial and royal
                        consulate at Philadelphia, at Harwood, September 12, 1897.</hi>
                </head>
                <p>Present: Those whose names are signed below.</p>
                <p>Thomas Racek, a naturalized American citizen, from Karacsony Mezzo, County Saros,
                    now residing in Harwood, Pa., on being questioned, makes the following
                    statement:</p>
                <p>“John Glavati, of Lattimer, came to my house on September 10 at 9 o’clock in the
                    morning and told me that he and his fellow-workmen in that place had decided to
                    join our strike if we would march to Lattimer. I went to Jacob Stiver and gave
                    him Glavati’s message, and we called the men together and decided to go in
                    procession to Lattimer, and we started on the same day at 1 o’clock.”</p>
                <p>Read, and, after the party had declared that he might suffer injury from making
                    the deposition and asked to be excused from signing it, the testimony was
                    closed.</p>
                <p>Official witness:</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">John Nemeth</hi>, <hi
                            rendition="#italic">Notary Public.</hi></signed><lb/><signed><hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">D. Thodorovich</hi>, <hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Chancellor</hi></signed>. [<hi rendition="#small-caps">l.
                    s.</hi>]</closer>
            </div>
